[38;2;124;124;124mThe Kolmogorov Extension Theorem (also known as the Kolmogorov Consistency [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mTheorem) is about the existence of a probability measure on a product space [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgiven a consistent family of finite-dimensional distributions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe classical Kolmogorov Extension Theorem states: Let $\{S_t\}_{t \in T}$ be a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mfamily of standard Borel spaces (or more specifically, $\mathbb{R}^n$ or Polish [0m[38;2;124;124;124mspaces), and let $\{\mu_{t_1,...,t_n}\}$ be a consistent family of finite-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional distributions. Then there exists a unique probability measure on the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mproduct space $\prod_{t \in T} S_t$ with the product $\sigma$-algebra that has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthese finite-dimensional distributions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key question is: does this hold without ANY assumptions on the topology of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe state space?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is False. The Kolmogorov Extension Theorem does NOT hold without [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtopological assumptions on the state space. The theorem requires the state [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspaces to be "nice" topological spaces — specifically, standard Borel spaces, or[0m[38;2;124;124;124mPolish spaces, or at least spaces that are Borel isomorphic to a subset of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$[0,1]$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe classical version assumes the state spaces are $\mathbb{R}$ (or $\mathbb{R}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m^n$), which are Polish. More general versions require the spaces to be standard [0m[38;2;124;124;124mBorel (i.e., Borel isomorphic to a Borel subset of a Polish space).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThere are counterexamples when the state space is an arbitrary measurable space [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwithout topological structure. For instance, if the state space is not a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstandard Borel space, the extension may fail to exist.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA classic counterexample: Let $S$ be a set with cardinality larger than the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuum, equipped with the $\sigma$-algebra of countable and co-countable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msets. One can construct a consistent family of finite-dimensional distributions [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat does not extend to a probability measure on the product.[0m

